Bastion Host:
A Bastion Host is an EC2 instance deployed in a public subnet. It is used by DevOps engineers and system administrators to securely SSH into Linux servers or RDP into Windows servers that are running in private subnets. 
It acts as a jump server and is used for server administration, troubleshooting, checking logs, restarting services, and deploying applications. It does not provide internet access to private instances.

NAT Gateway:
A NAT Gateway is an AWS managed service deployed in a public subnet. It allows EC2 instances in private subnets to access the internet for outbound traffic only.
It is commonly used for OS updates, downloading packages, pulling Docker images, accessing external APIs, and downloading Maven or NPM dependencies. It does not allow inbound connections from the internet.

Difference:
Bastion Host is used by administrators to access private EC2 instances using SSH or RDP.
NAT Gateway is used by private EC2 instances to access the internet without exposing them to inbound internet traffic.


Interview One-Liner:

Bastion Host provides secure administrative access to private EC2 instances, whereas NAT Gateway provides outbound internet connectivity for private EC2 instances.
